<header><a class="brand" href="/">anticipate<span>.</span></a><a class="back" href="/#series">NBA TIME MACHINE / THE SERIES ↗</a></header>
<main>
<section class="hero">
<div class="series"><b>02 / 10</b> NBA TIME MACHINE · EPISODE TWO</div>
<div class="eyebrow" style="margin-top:27px">ANTICIPATE ORIGINAL RESEARCH / THE MODERN POINT GUARD</div>
<h1>Isiah Thomas looks <em>surprisingly familiar.</em></h1>
<p class="dek">John Stockton was almost impossible to replicate. Zeke looked like he might be a different story. We went looking for the closest version of him in the modern NBA, and found a little more than we expected.</p>
<div class="byline"><span>BY <strong>DIVINE OSAS-EGBE</strong></span><span><strong>1984–85</strong> VS. <strong>2025–26</strong></span><span><strong>NBA TIME MACHINE 02</strong></span></div>
<figure class="lead-image"><img src="/images/isiah-thomas-pistons.jpg" alt="Isiah Thomas holding a basketball in a Detroit Pistons blue number 11 jersey" width="597" height="335" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" /><figcaption>Isiah Thomas, Detroit Pistons. Photograph supplied by the author; photographer credit to be confirmed.</figcaption></figure>
</section>
<div class="stats-strip" aria-label="Isiah Thomas 1984–85 season highlights">
 <div class="stat"><strong>21.2</strong><span>POINTS PER GAME</span></div>
 <div class="stat"><strong>13.9</strong><span>ASSISTS PER GAME<br/>NBA LEADER</span></div>
 <div class="stat"><strong>2.3</strong><span>STEALS PER GAME</span></div>
 <div class="stat"><strong>47.9%</strong><span>ASSIST PERCENTAGE</span></div>
</div>
<div class="body-grid">
<article class="prose">
<p class="opening">Last time, we went looking for a modern John Stockton. We found a few players who shared parts of his game, but nobody who really brought the whole thing together.</p>
<p>Stockton's numbers were oddly specific. A staggering amount of playmaking, elite efficiency for his era, and plenty of activity on defense, all from a relatively small point guard. You can find pieces of that combination today. Finding all of them in one player proved much harder.</p>
<p>Isiah Thomas felt like a different challenge.</p>
<p>Watch Zeke, and you can see a type of point guard that looks familiar: someone who can run the entire offense, but also decide, without much warning, that it's time to get his own shot. Plenty of modern guards are asked to do some version of that.</p>
<p>So is Isiah easier to match than Stockton? And if he is, who actually comes closest?</p>
<h2><span class="section-no">01 / STARTING IN DETROIT</span>The version of Zeke we're testing</h2>
<p>For this episode, we're using Isiah's <strong>1984–85 season</strong>, not the title-winning Detroit teams that came later. At 23, he averaged 21.2 points, an NBA-leading 13.9 assists, and 2.3 steals across 81 games. That's a lot of responsibility for a player still early in his career.</p>
<p>His advanced numbers are what make this investigation interesting. He assisted on an estimated <strong>47.9% of his teammates' baskets</strong> while on the floor, with a 24.0% usage rate. He also produced 25.5 points per 100 possessions.</p>
<p>So yes, he was an extraordinary passer. But he also had more scoring responsibility than the Stockton season we examined, and he wasn't nearly as efficient relative to his league.</p>
<div class="compare-small">
 <h3>Two very different 6-foot-1 guards</h3>
 <div class="mini-line"><span>Metric</span><span style="color:var(--lime)">STOCKTON '90</span><strong>ISIAH '85</strong></div>
 {[
  ['PTS / 100','23.0','25.5'],['USG%','20.6%','24.0%'],['AST%','57.4%','47.9%'],['rTS','+7.0 pp','−1.4 pp'],['STL%','3.5%','2.8%'],['Listed weight','170 lb','180 lb']
 ].map(row=><div class="mini-line"><span>{row[0]}</span><b>{row[1]}</b><strong>{row[2]}</strong></div>)}
 <p class="tiny">The scoring and usage gap is real, but it doesn't mean Stockton was only a passer. He averaged 17.2 points per game in his selected season. These are two distinct ways of running an offense.</p>
</div>
<p>That efficiency difference stands out. Isiah's true shooting was 52.9%, about 1.4 percentage points below the NBA's 1984–85 average. Stockton, in 1989–90, was seven points above his league average. We're not saying one number explains every part of their games. It does tell us the model is searching for a different profile this time.</p>
<div class="pull">Stockton was a playmaker who scored efficiently. Isiah was a playmaker who also took on more of the scoring burden.</div>
<p>I think there's a bigger difference here than the box score alone can explain. They're both undersized point guards, but Stockton's first instinct was usually to find the best shot in the flow of the offense, as efficiently as possible. That doesn't mean he couldn't score or that he only passed. It means he was usually looking to create the right opportunity before looking for his own.</p>
<p>Isiah could run an offense that way, too. But he was also willing to attack, take the tougher shot, and create something for himself when the possession called for it. He wasn't just finding shots for teammates. He was looking for ways to make the defense worry about <em>his</em> shot as well.</p>
<p>To me, <strong>Stockton's archetype is essentially dead in today's NBA</strong>: the small, relentlessly pass-first guard who can build an entire offensive identity around getting everyone else the best look. <strong>Isiah's archetype is dying, but it isn't gone.</strong> For better or worse, smaller point guards today often have to be much more aggressive about their own scoring while still creating for others. That's why Zeke feels closer to the modern game, even if finding another one of him isn't quite so simple.</p>
<h2><span class="section-no">02 / THE 2025–26 SEARCH</span>The closest modern match is another Isaiah</h2>
<p>We ran Isiah against the same 279 players from the 2025–26 season who logged at least 1,000 minutes. No career averages, no highlight-reel scouting grades. Just the same seven advanced statistical measures, plus height, weight, and a broad positional group.</p>
<p>The top result was <strong>Isaiah Collier</strong>.</p>
<p>I know. One Isiah, one Isaiah. But the names are the least interesting part of it.</p>
<p>Collier finished with an overall distance of <strong>0.811</strong>, and the strongest connection is something that worked against him in the Stockton episode: scoring efficiency. He and Zeke were both 1.4 percentage points below their respective league-average true shooting marks. Against Stockton's +7.0, that was a huge gap. Against Isiah, it's an exact match in our rounded data.</p>
<p>Collier also created plenty of offense for teammates, posting a 39.2% assist percentage. It's not Isiah's 47.9%, but it's much closer than many modern guards get. Their steal rates were 2.0% and 2.8%, respectively.</p>
<p>The physical comparison is where it gets less convincing. Collier is listed at 193 cm and 210 pounds. Zeke was listed at 185 cm and 180. Thirty pounds and eight centimetres aren't small differences for two guards.</p>
</article>
