<div class="body-grid" style="margin-top:0"><article class="prose">
<h2><span class="section-no">04 / MORE THAN ONE ANSWER</span>Porter looks closer in different ways</h2>
<p>Kevin Porter Jr. finished second at 0.829, just behind Collier. If you focus on points per 100 possessions, usage, relative efficiency, and steals, you could make a pretty convincing case for him.</p>
<p>Isiah: 25.5 points per 100 and 24.0% usage. Porter: 25.8 and 24.3%. Both finished with an rTS of −1.4 percentage points. Their steal percentages were 2.8 and 3.2. It's an oddly close group of numbers.</p>
<p>But Porter's assist rate was 33.8%, well below Zeke's 47.9%, and he's a bigger guard. Once again, the model finds resemblance without finding a duplicate.</p>
<p>Then there's Ryan Rollins in third at 0.871. His scoring and usage look close enough, and he's nearly the same weight as Isiah. But his 27.1% assist rate tells you how different their offensive responsibilities were.</p>
<p>Fourth-place De'Aaron Fox (0.874) and fifth-place Darius Garland (0.877) round out the group. Both produced more points per 100 possessions than Isiah, with lower assist rates. It's a recurring trade-off in these comparisons: we keep finding guards who look familiar as scorers, but not quite as creators.</p>
<div class="ranking"><h3>The original top five</h3>{players.map((p,i)=><div class="rankrow"><span class="num">{String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}</span><span>{p.name}</span><span class="score">{scoreFmt(p.distance)}</span></div>)}<p class="fine" style="margin:14px 0 0">Distance, not percentage similarity. Lower is closer under the 70/20/10 model.</p></div>
<p>Still, Isiah's best eligible match at 0.811 is much closer than Stockton's 1.300. With exactly the same model and modern sample, the general idea holds: <strong>Isiah's measurable profile translates more naturally to the modern player pool.</strong></p>
<p>But there's one name we haven't tested yet.</p>
</article><aside class="sidebar"><div class="sidebox"><h3>A SMALL DISTANCE IS NOT A TIE</h3><p>Model differences can move when you change the weighting, physical listings or chosen season. Don't read 0.811 as a precise measure of playing style.</p></div><div class="sidebox"><h3>MODEL LIMITATION</h3><p>STL% is defensive activity, not a verdict on who is the better defender. A higher usage rate isn't a complete measure of how aggressive a player looks with the ball.</p></div></aside></div>
<section class="bonus" id="trae">
 <div class="kicker">05 / THE WILDCARD · 2024–25, NOT PART OF THE ORIGINAL RANKING</div>
 <h2>What about Trae Young?</h2>
 <p>This is the comparison I kept coming back to. Trae isn't in the official 2025–26 top five because he didn't reach our 1,000-minute cutoff that season. But go back one year, to 2024–25, and his numbers start to look remarkably familiar.</p>
 <p>Trae averaged 31.4 points per 100 possessions, created an estimated 46.4% of his teammates' baskets, and had a 29.6% usage rate. Zeke's assist percentage was 47.9%. That's a gap of just 1.5 percentage points, much smaller than what we saw with any of our qualifying top-five matches.</p>
 <div class="score-pair"><div><div class="kicker">TRAE '24–25 VS. ISIAH '84–85</div><div class="large-score">0.809</div><div class="small-score">OUT-OF-SAMPLE COMPARISON DISTANCE</div></div><div class="vs">VS.</div><div><div class="kicker">BEST 2025–26 ELIGIBLE MATCH · COLLIER</div><div class="large-score" style="color:var(--lime)">0.811</div><div class="small-score">QUALIFIED-SEASON DISTANCE</div></div></div>
 <p><strong style="color:#fff">That's almost a tie.</strong> Trae just edges Collier using the same modern baseline, with his NBA-listed height of 188 cm and weight of 164 pounds. The margin is roughly 0.002 distance units, far too small to make a grand claim about who's the “real” Isiah.</p>
 <div class="bonus-visuals">
   <div><h3>Isiah vs. Trae: statistical radar</h3><svg class="radar bonus-shape" viewBox="0 0 410 410" role="img" aria-label="Radar chart comparing Isiah Thomas 1984–85 to Trae Young 2024–25">
    {Array.from({length:5},(_,i)=><polygon class="ring" points={ring(stats,(i+1)/5)} />)}
    {stats.map((f,i)=>{const line=endpoint(i,stats.length);const label=endpoint(i,stats.length,1.21);return <g><line class="axis" x1="205" y1="205" x2={line.x} y2={line.y}/><text x={label.x} y={label.y} text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="middle">{f.short}</text></g>})}
    <polygon class="legend-shape" points={polygon(isiah,stats)} /><polygon class="match-shape" points={polygon(trae,stats)} />
   </svg><div class="legend-key" style="justify-content:center"><span><i style="background:var(--lime)"></i>ISIAH</span><span><i style="background:var(--teal)"></i>TRAE</span></div></div>
   <div><h3>Where they're close, where they're not</h3><div class="mini-line line-head"><span>Metric</span><span style="color:var(--lime)">ISIAH</span><strong>TRAE</strong></div>
   {[
    ['AST%','47.9%','46.4%'],['PTS/100','25.5','31.4'],['USG%','24.0%','29.6%'],['rTS','−1.4 pp','−0.9 pp'],['STL%','2.8%','1.6%'],['Height','185 cm','188 cm'],['Weight','180 lb','164 lb']
   ].map(row=><div class="mini-line"><span>{row[0]}</span><b>{row[1]}</b><strong>{row[2]}</strong></div>)}
   <p class="diff">One is a relatively small, lighter modern scoring creator. The other is a high-assist guard with substantially more steals and a lower scoring usage rate.</p></div>
 </div>
 <p>The similarities are real. Both generated extraordinary assist numbers, and both scored at slightly below their respective league-average true shooting rates. But Trae took on more shooting responsibility, while Isiah posted a substantially higher steal percentage. That last number doesn't settle the defensive debate by itself.</p>
 <p>For accuracy, we calculated Trae's relative true shooting at approximately −0.9 percentage points using his 56.7% TS and the NBA's 57.6% league mark in 2024–25. That corrects an earlier estimate of −1.1. Importantly, Trae's tiny advantage over Collier is still present after that adjustment.</p>
</section>
<div class="body-grid" style="margin-top:0"><article class="prose">
<h2><span class="section-no">06 / THE POINT</span>Isiah feels familiar. That doesn't make him ordinary.</h2>
<p>After Stockton, I half expected another legend with a statistical profile nobody in the modern game could touch. Isiah gave us something more recognizable.</p>
<p>Collier resembles his combination of passing and below-average relative efficiency. Porter comes astonishingly close in scoring volume, steals and usage. Rollins, Fox and Garland each carry pieces of the offensive profile. Then Trae, from a different modern season, gets within touching distance of Zeke's assist rate while scoring far more aggressively.</p>
<p>Maybe that tells us something about how the position has evolved. The modern NBA asks its lead guards to create for everyone else and threaten the defense themselves. Isiah was doing a version of both more than forty years ago.</p>
<p>But I'd be careful calling him a modern point guard dropped into the past. Different rules, spacing, teammates and offensive priorities shape the numbers. And no radar chart measures how a defense reacts when a player changes speed or picks the perfect moment to attack.</p>
<p><strong>The model's official answer is Isaiah Collier. Trae Young is the fascinating outside challenger.</strong> Neither is a substitute for Zeke, but both help explain why his game still feels so recognizable.</p>
<p>Stockton was hard to find because his exact combination barely existed in our sample. Isiah was easier to find because his blend of scoring and playmaking is still at the centre of what many guards are asked to do.</p>
<p><strong>And that might be the more interesting kind of similarity.</strong></p>
<div class="methods"><h3>THE FINE PRINT / SOURCES</h3>
 <p><strong>Sample:</strong> 279 NBA players in 2025–26 with at least 1,000 minutes; one combined row for traded players. Historical reference: Isiah Thomas's 1984–85 regular season. Trae's 2024–25 season is a separately introduced extra comparison, not a 2025–26 ranked entry.</p>
 <p><strong>Model:</strong> seven standardized statistics averaged into a squared statistical distance (70% weight), listed height and weight (20%), and guard/forward/center position mismatch (10%). Overall distance is the square root of the weighted squared components. Modern z-score standard deviations come from the same 279-player pool for every comparison. This is not an estimate of skill, greatness, or percentage similarity.</p>
 <p><strong>Chart note:</strong> the radars use fixed visual scales, not the z-score metric; relative TS is player TS minus that season's league TS in percentage points. 3P% is left out because radically different shooting volume and shot selection make simple cross-era accuracy comparisons misleading.</p>
 <p><strong>Research sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.basketball-reference.com/players/t/thomais01.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Isiah historical stats</a> · <a href="https://www.nba.com/news/detroit-pistons-single-season-assists-per-game-leaders" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NBA: Detroit assist history</a> · <a href="https://www.basketball-reference.com/players/y/youngtr01.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Trae Young 2024–25 stats</a> · <a href="https://www.nba.com/player/1629027/trae-young/bio" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Trae listed size</a> · <a href="https://www.basketball-reference.com/leagues/NBA_stats_totals.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">2024–25 league TS</a> · <a href="https://github.com/mudia-OE/anticipate-nba-time-machine" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NBA Time Machine datasets, R code and methodology</a>.</p>
 <p><strong>Editorial limits:</strong> historical values have been checked for this article against the published source tables, but our wider ten-player database is still undergoing review. Listed heights and weights may not represent a particular season. Changing the model weights or season choice can change the rankings. Photo supplied by the author; photographer credit pending.</p>
